<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><opml version="2.0"><head><title>Alpha Hours: Business model</title></head><body><outline text="Business Model and Operations"><outline text="The membership"><outline text="A member gets: two one-hour center visits a week at fixed slots, Timeback at home every day, both core subjects, the placement assessment at enrollment, a mastery report and weeks-to-grade-level projection every month, Alpha bucks and the Emporium, and a parent conversation with the Lead Guide every month for Considering and Path to yes families and every quarter for Stay families."/><outline text="Tiers, premium markets: Standard membership (two visits a week) $1,750 a month. Plus (three visits) $2,250. Daily (Monday to Thursday plus Saturday) $3,000. Standard-tier markets: $1,250, $1,600, $2,150. Enrollment fee $750, which covers the placement assessment and the first month&#x27;s Emporium balance. No sibling discount. Current Alpha families 5 percent off. Annual prepay accepted at list, no discount. Thirty days&#x27; notice to cancel. Month-to-month, auto-renew."/></outline><outline text="The visit"><outline text="A visit is 60 minutes. Arrival and check-in in the first five minutes: the child collects their device, opens Timeback, and the floor guide confirms the day&#x27;s target. Two 25-minute mastery sessions, one math and one reading, with a five-minute break between. Alpha bucks are earned on XP and on completing the visit&#x27;s target. Shoutouts and dismissal in the last five minutes: the guide names two or three mastery wins in the room, out loud, every visit."/><outline text="Between visits, the child does one 25-minute Timeback session at home on each of the other days, tracked in the same account. The Lead Guide sees a home-practice streak for every member and the floor guide mentions it at check-in. Alpha bucks are earned at home too, at half rate."/></outline><outline text="Center hours and slots"><outline text="Monday to Thursday: visit slots at 3:30, 4:30, 5:30 and 6:30 pm. Saturday: 9:00, 10:00, 11:00 and 12:00. Friday closed to members; the Lead Guide uses Friday for placement assessments, parent conversations and path-to-yes re-evaluations. Twenty slots a week per seat. A family chooses two fixed slots at enrollment and can move them with a week&#x27;s notice when space allows. Current Alpha students take the 3:30 slot and walk down the hall."/></outline><outline text="Capacity"><outline text="Members per seat is twenty slots divided by two visits: ten. A 20-seat room serves 200 members. Pilot seats: New York 100, Austin 100, Miami 60, Chicago 60, Greenwich 40, Boston 40, a total of 400 seats and 4,000 members at capacity. Every campus can add seats by opening another room; the constraint is demand and floor guides, not space."/></outline><outline text="The lanes in operation"><outline text="Every family answers one question at enrollment: are you considering Alpha full-time? Three answers: no, we love our school (Stay); yes, we are deciding (Considering); we applied and were told not yet (Path to yes, set by admissions, not the family). Current Alpha families are flagged from the roster. The lane is on the roster, the Lead Guide sees it, and the data can move a family between lanes: a Stay family whose child&#x27;s mastery map shows a two-grade jump gets the Considering conversation whether they asked for it or not, and a Considering family who declines twice moves to Stay and stops getting the pitch."/><outline text="The Stay family gets the monthly report and a quarterly conversation. The Considering family gets the same report plus the weeks-to-grade-level projection at full Alpha and the structured conversion conversation, which fires only when published thresholds are met. The Path to yes family gets the report scored against their roadmap thresholds and an invitation to apply the month they clear them."/></outline><outline text="Motivation and incentives"><outline text="The child walks in tired at 4:30 pm after six hours of school. Alpha&#x27;s motivation model carries into the center unchanged: Alpha bucks for XP and for completing the visit target, earned at the center and at home, spent in the Emporium. Shoutouts every visit. The monthly report goes to the parent with three numbers: where the child is, where they were last month, and what is next."/></outline><outline text="Placement and the honest number"><outline text="Every member starts with a placement assessment: MAP math and reading, and a two-hour Timeback block that measures learning velocity. Children are placed slightly below where they test so the first win comes in the first week. The placement report is the first thing the family receives and, for most of them, the first real number they have ever seen for their child."/></outline><outline text="Team"><outline text="Central: Head of Alpha Hours ($400,000), Operations VP ($200,000, remote), Admissions Integration Lead ($150,000, owns lane routing and the conversion process with every campus admissions team), AI-first engineer ($200,000, owns the roster, reporting and Timeback integration), Finance and compliance ($150,000), tooling $250,000. About $1.35M a year."/><outline text="Per campus: Lead Guide (full-time, $100,000) runs the center, reads the data, holds every parent conversation and every re-evaluation. Center Coordinator ($60,000) runs enrollment, scheduling, billing and the Emporium. Floor guides at one per 20 seats, part-time at $30 an hour, 22 hours a week, about $32,000 a year each, scheduled to fill. Alpha High students may serve as paid floor assistants at one per 40 seats, at the campus&#x27;s discretion."/></outline><outline text="Compliance"><outline text="Alpha Hours is instruction, not care. Filings before launch: New York (OCFS Policy 16-1: tutoring and classes exemption; multi-purpose programs registered case by case), Texas (HHSC Form 2820 exemption for programs operated by an accredited private school), Florida (s. 402.305 school-site exemption), Illinois, Connecticut, Massachusetts. Background screening for every adult in the room regardless of exemption. Cognia systemwide accreditation covers the campuses. Legal budget $60,000 in the launch budget."/></outline><outline text="Pricing logic"><outline text="The price is quoted per month, never per hour, and anchored on the outcome: a mastery map, a learning velocity, and a weeks-to-grade-level number no school or tutor can produce. At $1,750 a month a family pays less than one hour a week of an elite tutor and gets two guided hours plus daily practice in both subjects. A year is $21,000, about a third of full Alpha tuition in premium markets. Waitlist repricing: two consecutive months with a waitlist reprices new memberships by $100 a month. Annual step: list price rises $100 a month every September for new members; existing members hold their rate for twelve months from enrollment."/></outline></outline></body></opml>